courses.learning_outcomes:
Upon the completion of the course, students will be able to –

1) possess a basic knowledge of the historical contexts for the development of modern China, including important thinkers, scholars, writers, and political leaders who have made significant contributions to national development;

2) have a basic familiarity with political milestones in the development from imperial to modern China, including landmark events that have affected the historical process of modernization;

3) have developed some understanding of the great transformations in Chinese society since the Republican revolution, with reference to political, socio-economic and cultural development;

4) have acquired a conceptual understanding of the development of modern China from the Chinese perspective, in comparison with alternative perspectives;

5) possess a grasp of the key concepts and the framework of discourse that underpin government programmes and policies and drive development in China today, and how they relate to the strategic development of the nation and the HKSAR;

6) have gained a deepened understanding of the multi-faceted complexities of nation building and modernization in China, and the challenges and achievements of the nation;

7) appreciate the fact that one’s view of China’s historical path could be critically shaped by conceptual frameworks rooted in different intellectual traditions, and the importance of being able to critically examine different interpretations of China’s modernization.

courses.course_syllabus:
The syllabus of this course comprises of four main areas of instruction:

(1) Theories of modernization and social change pertaining to the study of Chinese society;

(2) Aspects of cultural changes as in the way of life, the social psychology, and the religious beliefs of the Chinese people;

(3) Aspects of institutional transformation as in the family, the state, and the economic system;

(4) Aspects of social structural changes as in modes of social stratification and modes of production.

While the first area sets the theoretical direction of the course as a whole, the other three areas address matters of more topical concerns.In each case, however, the discussion will focus upon the variegated trajectories that led “from tradition to modernity”.
